 ## Overview
-Normal Alignment Regularization is a geometric regularization technique used in differentiable volume rendering and 3D Gaussian Splatting to align the learned scene representation with the underlying physical surface [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of misaligned Gaussians by penalizing deviations between rendered normals and external geometric constraints, such as planar surfaces, rendered depth buffers, or prior normal maps [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods by focusing on general surface alignment via external constraints rather than specific alignment to ray directions [4202-normal-ray-alignment-loss.md] or ground-truth normal labels [4232-normal-ground-truth-alignment-loss.md] [G].
